From: CoolFreeze23 <34906576+CoolFreeze23@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 20 Oct 2026 12:00:00 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] wifi: ath12k: ignore a reset work of a device being removed

The MHI RDDM callback tests UNREGISTERING without ag->mutex (taking the
mutex there could wait behind a group power-up), so a reset work it
queues can start after remove cancelled the work. Return from such a run
before it touches the device; nothing waits for a device being removed.
Say so in the comment in ath12k_ahb_remove_prepare().
---
diff --git a/drivers/net/wireless/ath/ath12k/ahb.c b/drivers/net/wireless/ath/ath12k/ahb.c
--- a/drivers/net/wireless/ath/ath12k/ahb.c
+++ b/drivers/net/wireless/ath/ath12k/ahb.c
@@ -1400,7 +1400,9 @@ static void ath12k_ahb_remove_prepare(st
 	 * Under ag->mutex, which the group recovery paths hold while they
 	 * test this bit and queue reset_work, so they cannot queue it after
 	 * the cancel below. A reset work must not outlive the device: it
-	 * locks ab->ag->mutex, and ath12k_core_deinit() clears ab->ag.
+	 * locks ab->ag->mutex, and ath12k_core_deinit() clears ab->ag. The
+	 * MHI RDDM callback tests the bit without the mutex; a run it queues
+	 * after the cancel returns at once (ath12k_core_reset()).
 	 */
 	mutex_lock(&ab->ag->mutex);
 	set_bit(ATH12K_FLAG_UNREGISTERING, &ab->dev_flags);
diff --git a/drivers/net/wireless/ath/ath12k/core.c b/drivers/net/wireless/ath/ath12k/core.c
--- a/drivers/net/wireless/ath/ath12k/core.c
+++ b/drivers/net/wireless/ath/ath12k/core.c
@@ -1885,6 +1885,14 @@ static void ath12k_core_reset(struct wor
 		return;
 	}
 
+	/*
+	 * The MHI RDDM callback tests UNREGISTERING without ag->mutex, so a
+	 * run can be queued after remove cancelled the work. Nothing waits
+	 * for a device being removed: return without touching it further.
+	 */
+	if (test_bit(ATH12K_FLAG_UNREGISTERING, &ab->dev_flags))
+		return;
+
 	if (!(test_bit(ATH12K_FLAG_QMI_FW_READY_COMPLETE, &ab->dev_flags))) {
 		ath12k_warn(ab, "firmware crashed before QMI ready; recovery skipped (dev flags 0x%lx)\n",
 			    ab->dev_flags);
